NAME
    packages - installing add-ons and writing your own

DESCRIPTION
    Add-ons in TOS are packages, managed by 'pkg' (the legacy module manager was
    retired in v1.3.1). A package is a directory with a package.lua manifest
    plus its files laid out at their install paths.

INSTALLING
    The bundled add-ons (games, tape tools, calc, mail, the cluster packages
    and more) ship on the Optional Utilities disks. Insert one and run
    'pkg install' with no argument: a picker lists every package in the set,
    including those on a disk that is not inserted, and asks you to swap
    disks when it needs to. 'pkg install <name>' installs one directly.
    The disks carry no installer of their own; the picker is part of TOS.
    See 'man pkg' for the rest.

WRITING A PACKAGE
    A minimal manifest:

        return {
          name = "mytool", version = "1.0.0", kind = "command",
          files = { "/usr/modules/mytool/init.lua" },
          commands = { mytool = "/usr/modules/mytool/init.lua" },
          capabilities = { "fs.read", "fs.write", "component" },
        }

    The command entry returns a module table:

        return { commands = { mytool = function(args, o) ... end } }

    It runs in a sandbox built from the ALLOWLISTED capabilities (fs.read,
    fs.write, component, compat.io, load, net, swap, peripheral.*). The "legacy"
    capability (raw os/io) can never be requested by a manifest.

    valid kinds: command, program, app, lib, service, runtime, driver, theme.
    Declare hashes = { ["/usr/.../init.lua"] = "<64hex>" } for SHA-256
    verification at install and at run.

SEE ALSO
